vice president gore thank you very much  for talking to us it's just two weeks  until cop 26 these crucial u.n climate  talks in glasgow the u.s climate envoy  has said they're probably going to fall  short how worried are you  well i think there's a lot of momentum  and i think it's likely to increase  the president of my country president  biden has made a commitment to double  the u.s contribution to the climate  finance that has long been promised to  the developing countries of the world  and the president of china xi jinping  has made a very important commitment to  completely stop the financing of all the  overseas  coal plants you have said that you're  hopeful about china it is the biggest  polluter in the world and it has also  signaled that it may actually build more  coal plants domestically and we still  don't even know if the president is  going to be attending cop26 is china  holding the talks to ransom  i wouldn't put it that way at all um i  would say that in my experience china  plans its work and then works its plan  and  when  china made its uh surprise commitment  one year ago  to reach net zero by 2060. many  people were  excited and pleased but simultaneously  disappointed that the date was so far  out but with a country of 1.4 billion  people  the majority of whom live below what  would be described in western countries  as the poverty level  it's a a big task for them so you're  still hopeful on china i am still  hopeful they just announced uh  two days ago a massive new  uh solar  installation almost as large as all of  the solar in india  and i i choose to be optimistic about it  but  china needs to do more uh every country  including my own needs to do more you  talk about doing more the queen this  week said that she is irritated by  leaders who are talking but not doing  is she right well first of all i felt  sympathy for her majesty to have what  was obviously a private conversation  picked up with boom mics and then  amplified but yes of course she's right  in terms of the us president joe biden  is sending nearly half his cabinet to  cop to cop26 he's trying to position the  country as a as a leader again and  particularly on climate and yet the us  is historically the biggest polluter and  in three years time at the next  presidential elections we may see the  paris agreement repealed again why  should anyone listen to the united  states some 55 colleges and universities  host  young republican clubs that have banded  together to demand that their party  change its position on climate the young  people are leading in my country and in  every country now and there is now a  growing demand from the grassroots  activists regardless of political party  and i think that's going to make a big  difference in the elections ahead what  are your worst fears for cop26  well i choose not to focus on  anxiety and fear about it but rather to  to be optimistic there is a lot at stake  well um  the tensions between the u.s and china  on other issues have made it  a little bit more difficult for the two  largest emitters to be aligned as they  were just before the paris agreement  but i'm still optimistic and hopeful and  the announcements just within the last  three weeks that i referred to  buttress that optimism the uk has  positioned itself as a climate leader  this year as it hope hosts these talks  cop 26 and yet greta tunberg has said  that uk is a climate villain how would  you describe the uk  well i i i first of all the uk has made  a a commitment to 68 reductions in  greenhouse gas emissions by 2030 which  is a very impressive commitment if  there's follow-through then that will  make the uk a leader such as canceling  this new  oil field development that we've heard  much about so you're saying it should be  cancelled and no more licences for oil  in the north sea i'm not a citizen of  the uk and it's not my place to  interfere in your politics but yes  that's my view  the longer we continue to dawdle  by remaining so dependent on fossil  fuels the harder it will be to make this  change so i think we need to move faster  and that to me  is consistent with just stopping these  new oil and gas and coal developments  these talks are all about trust does  boris johnson the uk prime minister  inspire the trust of other world leaders  well i think the commitment to the  massive reductions in co2 emissions by  2030 is is very impressive it's more  than the european union more than my  country more than almost any country but  commitment and action are very different  so does he inspire trust with his action  certainly true uh but the commitment can  lead to action if the people of the uk  continue to put pressure on the  government to follow through so why are  you saying yes he does inspire faith  globally  well i i think that there have been some  impressive policy changes  i will also say again the uk needs to do  more as my own country needs to do much  more  and it's been 15 years since your  seminal climate film an inconvenient  truth in it you said that you have been  trying to raise awareness of climate  change  but you felt that you had failed it's 15  years on  emissions are going up extreme weather  is getting worse do you still feel that  you've failed  well um  i feel that like everyone i need to do  more  and i've gained an appreciation for how  difficult this challenge is and the  period of time necessary to make this  historic transition  but i i think the momentum that has  emerged in the last several years since  the paris agreement  has been very impressive and i'm more  optimistic today than i was just a few  years ago
 

